The following is a code with two threads, producer and consumer, that can run in parallel. Further, S and Q are binary semaphores quipped with the standard P and V operations. semaphore S = 1, Q = 0; 
integer x;

producer:                   consumer:
while (true) do             while (true) do
    P(S);                       P(Q);
    x = produce ();             consume (x);
    V(Q);                       V(S);
done                        done Which of the following is TRUE about the program above?

Consider the code fragment written in C below : void f (int n)
{ 
  if (n <=1)  {
   printf ("%d", n);
  }
  else {
   f (n/2);
   printf ("%d", n%2);
  }
} What does f(173) print?

Consider the code fragment written in C below : void f (int n)
{ 
    if (n <= 1)  {
        printf ("%d", n);
    }
    else {
        f (n/2);
        printf ("%d", n%2);
    }
} Which of the following implementations will produce the same output for f(173) as the above code? P1 void f (int n)
{ 
    if (n/2)  {
        f(n/2);
    }
    printf ("%d", n%2);
} P2 void f (int n)
{ 
